\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The covariant Wilson operator with lattice spacing}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Use the \vocabk{field space and unitary shifts}{fdip-009O}
#{\mathcal H=\ell^2(\Lambda)\otimes S\otimes F} and
#{T_j^U}, with #{\dim_{\mathbb C}S=2^n} and
#{\dim_{\mathbb C}F>0}. A lattice spacing #{a>0}
rescales the differences, while #{x\in\Lambda} remains an
integer site label. Define
##{\begin{aligned}
D_{a,U}&=a^{-1}D_U,\\
L_{a,U}&=-a^{-2}\sum_{j=1}^n
 \bigl(2\bbI -T_j^U-(T_j^U)^{-1}\bigr).
\end{aligned}}
For #{r\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}}, the massless
\newvocab{Wilson operator} in the skew Dirac convention is
##{D_{W,a,U}=D_{a,U}-\frac{ra}{2}L_{a,U}.}
The usual positive Wilson coefficient is #{r>0}. For #{a=1}
this is #{D_U-(r/2)L_{1,U}}. #{-L_{a,U}} is the
nearest-neighbor second difference, not the negative square of
the centered first difference.}

\p{Each #{T_j^U} is unitary, so
##{2\bbI -T_j^U-(T_j^U)^{-1}
 =(\bbI -T_j^U)^{\dagger}(\bbI -T_j^U).}
Consequently #{L_{a,U}} is self-adjoint and #{-L_{a,U}} is nonnegative,
whereas #{D_{a,U}} is [skew-adjoint](fdip-009P).
For the internal gauge transformation #{\mathcal G} of that
proposition, conjugating every shift and its inverse gives
##{\begin{aligned}
L_{a,U^g}&=\mathcal G L_{a,U}\mathcal G^{-1},\\
D_{W,a,U^g}&=\mathcal G D_{W,a,U}\mathcal G^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
This defines a covariant operator for all the supplied unitary
connections. The Fourier kernel theorem below assumes the
trivial connection.}

\p{For #{r=1}, this is the second-derivative addition in
\citet{Section 9.3, Equation (9.15), p. 21}{wingate2025lattice}:
#{-L_a=-\sum_j\cov_j^+\cov_j^-} in the free difference
notation. The coefficient #{ra/2}, rather than #{r/2}, is
required when #{L_a} contains #{a^{-2}}. A paired Grassmann
quadratic form may use this finite endomorphism as its kernel, as in
\ref{fdip-008H}. Gauge covariance and the Wilson coefficient
alone supply neither a positive probability density nor a
fermionic reflection-positive transfer construction.}
